course: world
lesson: 181
title: Trick questions that catch quick thinkers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Trick questions are built to catch quick thinkers: they get you starting the sum before you have read all the words.
explain: "A farmer has 17 sheep. All but 9 run away. How many are left?" Mia does a perfect 17 − 9 = 8, but "all but 9" means "all except 9", so 9 are left. Right sum, wrong question.
explain: Some hide a false idea inside the question: roosters don't lay eggs and electric trains make no smoke, so before you answer "which way?", check that the question makes sense.
explain: Others hide a step everyone skips (in a dark room you light the match first) or an idea nobody actually said (who said the dog's rope was tied to anything?).
explain: The cure is slow and simple: read every word, ask "what did it actually say?", then answer. Getting caught is normal; laugh at the trick, not at the person, and remember it next time.
fact: A kilogram of feathers weighs exactly the same as a kilogram of bricks; the feathers just take up much more space.
fact: If you overtake the runner in second place, you take second place; the leader is still ahead of you.
fact: All 12 months have at least 28 days; only February has just 28 (or 29 in a leap year).
fact: "All but 9" means "all except 9", so if all but 9 sheep run away, 9 sheep are left.
fact: Roosters are male chickens and don't lay eggs.
fact: Electric trains don't burn fuel on board, so they make no smoke.
fact: In "Mike's mum has three children: April, May and...", the third child is Mike.
fact: Three pills, one every half hour, starting now, are taken at 0, 30 and 60 minutes, so they are all gone in one hour.
fact: Counting the moments instead of the gaps between them (or the other way round) is called the fence-post error.
fact: Two fathers and two sons can be just three people: a grandfather, his son and his grandson, because the middle one is both a father and a son.
fact: In a dark room with one match, you light the match first.
fact: A dog on a 3-metre rope can reach a bone 5 metres away if the other end of the rope isn't tied to anything.
fact: Words people use: "sus" means suspicious; "cooked" (young people) means beaten, done for; "clever clogs" (UK) is a joking name for a know-it-all; "pet" (North of England) means dear; "our kid" (Manchester) means my brother or sister; "bro" means brother or mate.
exercise: Uncle Steve asks, "What's heavier, a kilo of feathers or a kilo of bricks?" What is the answer? | They weigh the same, one kilogram each | The bricks, because bricks are heavy | The feathers, because there are more of them | It depends on the size of the bag
exercise: Why do people expect the feathers to be lighter? | A kilo of feathers takes up much more space | Feathers have no weight at all, they just float | Air pushes feathers up into the sky | Feathers lose weight when you lift them
exercise: In a race, Ellie overtakes the girl in second place. What place is Ellie in now? | Second | First | Third | Last
exercise: The teacher asks, "How many months have 28 days?" What is the answer? | All 12 | Only February | Two | Eleven
exercise: Grandad: "A farmer has 17 sheep. All but 9 run away." How many sheep are left? | 9 | 8 | 17 | 26
exercise: What does "all but 9" mean? | All except 9 | All, plus 9 more | Only 9 of them ran | 9 fewer than all of them
exercise: Dad: "A rooster lays an egg on top of a pointy roof. Which side does the egg roll down?" | Neither; roosters don't lay eggs | The side the wind is blowing towards | The steeper side of the pointy roof | The side of the roof facing the sun
exercise: Mum: "An electric train goes north and the wind blows south. Which way does the smoke go?" | There is no smoke; electric trains don't make any | South, along with the wind | North, along with the train | Straight up into the sky
exercise: Auntie: "Mike's mum has three children. The first is April, the second is May. What's the third called?" | Mike | June | July | March
exercise: The nurse: "Three pills, take one every half hour. How long until they're all gone?" | One hour | An hour and a half | Half an hour | Two hours
exercise: Why isn't the pill answer an hour and a half? | The first pill is taken straight away, at 0 minutes | Pills dissolve faster than people think | The nurse takes one pill herself | Half an hour is only twenty minutes
exercise: "Two fathers and two sons went fishing. They caught three fish and each got one fish." How? | They were a grandfather, his son and his grandson | Someone lied about how many fish there were | One of the fish was cut into two halves | One of them took a fish from the shop
exercise: "You walk into a dark room with one match. There's a candle, a lamp and a fireplace. What do you light first?" | The match | The candle | The lamp | The fireplace
exercise: "A dog is tied to a 3-metre rope. A bone is 5 metres away. The dog gets the bone." How? | The other end of the rope isn't tied to anything | The rope stretched to 5 metres long | The bone rolled closer to the dog | The dog has a 2-metre neck
exercise: What is the best habit against trick questions? | Read every word before you start to answer | Answer as fast as you possibly can, before others | Always pick the biggest number | Always do a take-away sum
test: Josh shouts "Bricks!" His sister: "He's been cooked by a feather." What did Josh get wrong? | A kilo is a kilo, so they weigh the same | Feathers are really heavier than bricks, kilo for kilo | Bricks are lighter when they are wet | Nothing; bricks are always heavier
test: Ellie's brother: "Bro thinks overtaking is teleporting." What should Ellie have told her mum? | "I came second." | "I came first." | "I came third." | "I didn't finish."
test: Finn scratches his head: "January's got 31... so it's got 28 in it too. Ohhh." So how many months have 28 days? | All 12 | One | Four | Seven
test: Grandad: "You did the sum perfect, pet, just the wrong sum." Seventeen sheep, all but 9 run off. How many are left? | 9 | 8 | 0 | 17
test: Kai: "That's so sus, Dad." Dad: "Roosters are boys, Kai." What was the trap? | The question had a false idea built into it | The wind was blowing the wrong way for the egg | The roof wasn't pointy enough for an egg to roll | Hens are the ones that lay eggs on pointy roofs
test: What would happen if the train in Mum's riddle were an old steam train going north, with the wind blowing south? | Then there would be smoke, and it would drift south | There would still be no smoke at all | The smoke would always go north with the train | The train couldn't move against the wind
test: Ruby: "June, obviously!" Auntie: "Whose mum is it?" What is the answer? | Mike | June | Ruby | April
test: The nurse: "First one now, second at half past, third at the hour. Done in an hour, clever clogs." With 4 pills, one every half hour, starting now, how long until they're gone? | 1 hour 30 minutes | 2 hours | 1 hour | 2 hours 30 minutes
test: Amira's grandpa, his son Dad, and Dad's son Omar go fishing. How many fathers are in the boat? | Two | Three | One | Four
test: Big sister in Manchester: "Our kid tried to light the candle first. With his thoughts." What should come first? | The match | The candle | The lamp | Whatever is closest
test: In a cartoon the dog's rope stretches like chewing gum until he reaches the bone. Put the cartoon right. | Nobody said the rope was tied to anything | Real ropes stretch two metres easily when pulled hard | Dogs grow longer when they are hungry | The bone was only 3 metres away
exam: It is given that a farmer possesses seventeen sheep and that all but nine of them abscond. Ascertain the number of sheep remaining in the farmer's possession. | Nine | Eight | Seventeen | Twenty-six
exam: Determine the number of calendar months which contain twenty-eight days or more. | Twelve | One | Two | Eleven
exam: A patient is directed to take three pills, the first immediately and each subsequent pill thirty minutes after the preceding one. Compute the time elapsed until the final pill has been taken. | One hour | One hour and thirty minutes | Thirty minutes | Two hours
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a runner who overtakes the competitor in second place. | The runner thereby occupies second place | The runner thereby occupies first place | The runner thereby occupies third place | The runner's position cannot be determined
